CHATR's architectural thesis rests on three foundational axioms:
1. Reality over Acknowledgment: Modern business software is fundamentally decoupled from physical execution; true completion requires verified post-state observation rather than optimistic API responses;
2. Policy Precedes Execution: In an enterprise operating system, policy invariants strictly govern execution, superseding raw conversational prompts;
3. One Unified Reality: Collapsing fragmented departmental databases into a single living Organization Graph eliminates data synchronization latency and eliminates manual cross-app copy-pasting.

Synthesis Framework:
1. Identify underlying methodology differences (e.g. Forrester Wave vendor scoring criteria vs Gartner Magic Quadrant execution axes);
2. Compare sample sizes, surveyed company revenue brackets, and regional coverage;
3. Highlight points of consensus (e.g. both confirm 35% CAGR in enterprise AI);
4. Explicitly present conflicting projections with their respective methodological assumptions.

Whitepaper Structure:
1. Executive Summary: The macro transition from generative text suggestions to deterministic multi-app executions;
2. The Problem: The $4.2B productivity drain of manual SaaS context-switching;
3. Literature Review: Comparative evaluation of conversational chat (Layer 7 UI) vs intent execution kernels (Layer 4 OS);
4. Enterprise Architecture: Execution Definition Language (EDL), Zero-Copy connectors, and 1-Click Audit Traces;
5. Measurable Business Outcomes: 80% reduction in cycle time.

Citation Standards:
1. Document Path: Explicit relative filepath (e.g. `docs/00-Foundations/DECISION_THEORY.md`);
2. Section Anchor: Exact markdown header title;
3. Content Verifiability: Every factual assertion must link to an explicit source quote or quantitative data point in the repository;
4. Absence Declaration: If a queried fact is not present in the indexed corpus, state explicitly: 'Source not found in provided documentation.'

1. Known Knowns: Verified system behavior and documented APIs;
2. Known Unknowns: Identified risks with measurable probabilities (e.g. server hardware failure rates);
3. Unknown Knowns: Latent organizational knowledge that is siloed and unindexed;
4. Unknown Unknowns: Emergent black-swan systemic failures (e.g. multi-cloud cascading DNS outages).
